@meta (version: "1.0", title: "A fictional reading report", locale: "en-US")

# A neighborhood reading report

![Cozy reading nook with books by a window](assets/8fdb90d5752dad93797e210042b5a266b99e87ffe530c6f80ccee1de493df246.webp)

A community reading room wants to compare four opening days. These numbers are
fictional. Change the filters to ask which days clear a threshold; the summary,
chart and table all use the same selected rows.

@toggle include_weekends = false (label: "Include weekends")
@slider minimum = 0 (min: 0, max: 200, step: 5, label: "Minimum readings")
@choice selected_day = "Mon" (options: ["Mon", "Tue", "Sat", "Sun"], label: "Day to inspect")

@data reading
day:text | readings:number | audio:number | weekend:boolean
Mon | 120 | 30 | false
Tue | 160 | 45 | false
Sat | 95 | 40 | true
Sun | 80 | 25 | true
@end

@view visible = reading (
  where: (include_weekends or not row.weekend) and row.readings >= minimum,
  sort: "readings",
  descending: true,
)
@view detail = reading (where: row.day == selected_day)
@calc total = sum(visible, "readings")
@calc average = if(count(visible) > 0, mean(visible, "readings"), 0)

## The current picture

There are **{{ count(visible) }} days**, **{{ total }} readings**, and an average
of **{{ fixed(average, 1) }} readings per selected day**.

@chart bar visible (
  x: "day", y: ["readings", "audio"], select: selected_day,
  title: "Readings and audio sessions on the selected days", yLabel: "Sessions",
)

The selected day is **{{ selected_day }}**, with
**{{ sum(detail, "readings") }} readings in the original data**.
Selection remains available even if the current filter hides that day.

@chart donut visible (x: "day", y: "readings", title: "Share within the selected days")

@details "Read the selected rows"
@table visible (title: "The same rows used by the report")
@end

@when count(visible) == 0
No rows match these choices. Reduce the minimum or include weekends.
@end

## A useful question

Does adding the weekend change the pattern, or simply add more observations?
Use the toggle and threshold before interpreting either figure.
